from core import L
L(0,'propositions','Propositional logic and proof','Propositional logic;Truth tables;Implication;Logical equivalence','Comfort with true/false statements and basic algebra.',
'A proposition has a definite truth value. Logic asks whether the structure of an argument is valid, independently of the subject matter. First translate sentences into symbols; then reason about all possible assignments, not just one convenient example.',
'''Connectives and implication::AND is true only when both operands are true; OR is inclusive and false only when both are false. Negation reverses the truth value. P→Q is equivalent to ¬P∨Q: the promise fails only when P is true and Q is false. P↔Q requires equal truth values. XOR requires unequal values.
||Equivalence and normal forms::Two expressions are equivalent if their truth-table columns match for every assignment. De Morgan’s laws change ¬(P∧Q) to ¬P∨¬Q and ¬(P∨Q) to ¬P∧¬Q. Distribute AND over OR to obtain disjunctive normal form, or OR over AND for conjunctive normal form. A tautology is always true; a contradiction is always false; a satisfiable expression is true for at least one assignment.
||Checking an argument::An argument with premises A₁,…,Aₖ and conclusion C is valid if (A₁∧…∧Aₖ)→C is a tautology. To refute validity, find an assignment making all premises true and the conclusion false. The contrapositive ¬Q→¬P is equivalent to P→Q; the converse Q→P is not generally equivalent.''',
'''Show why P→Q and its converse differ::Choose P=false and Q=true.~P→Q is true because its antecedent is false.~Q→P is false because its antecedent is true and consequent false. A single counterexample disproves equivalence.
||Test: P→Q, Q→R, therefore P→R::Assume all premises true and try to make P→R false: P=true, R=false.~P→Q forces Q=true.~Q→R then forces R=true, a contradiction. No counterexample exists; the argument is valid.''',
'Necessary and sufficient are directional: P sufficient for Q means P→Q.||“Only if Q” introduces Q as a necessary condition.||A true implication does not mean both P and Q are true.',
'P→Q ≡ ¬P∨Q||Contrapositive preserves meaning; converse need not.||n variables give 2ⁿ truth-table rows.',
[('How many rows are needed for a truth table with 4 independent variables?',16,'Each variable has two possible values.','Multiply 2×2×2×2=16 assignments.'),('For how many assignments to P,Q is P→Q true?',3,'Identify its sole false case.','TT, FT and FF make the implication true. TF makes it false, so 3 assignments work.')])
L(0,'quantifiers','First-order logic and quantifiers','First order logic;Predicates;Universal quantifier;Existential quantifier','Propositional logic; understand a variable and a set.',
'A predicate becomes a proposition when its variables are given values or bound by quantifiers. The domain matters: “every number has a multiplicative inverse” changes truth when zero is included. Quantifier order expresses who gets to choose a value first.',
'''Meaning and scope::∀x P(x) requires P for every domain element; ∃x P(x) requires at least one witness. A bound variable lies within a quantifier’s scope; a free variable remains unspecified. Write brackets to show scope. “Every student knows some language” is ∀s ∃l Knows(s,l); it does not promise the same language for everyone.
||Negation::Negating ∀ changes it to ∃ and negates the predicate: ¬∀x P(x) ≡ ∃x ¬P(x). Similarly ¬∃x P(x) ≡ ∀x ¬P(x). For multiple quantifiers apply this from the outside inward. Negating a conjunction or implication inside the predicate still requires propositional rules.
||Order and witnesses::∃y∀x R(x,y) selects one y that works for all x. ∀x∃y R(x,y) permits y to depend on x. The former implies the latter on the usual nonempty domains, but not conversely. To disprove a universal statement provide a counterexample; to prove an existential statement provide a witness.''',
'''Negate “every student passed at least one exam”::Formalize as ∀s∃e Passed(s,e).~Move the negation inward: ∃s∀e ¬Passed(s,e).~Meaning: there is a student who passed no exams, not merely a student who failed one.
||Compare quantifiers for x+y=0 over integers::For ∀x∃y, choose y=−x. This works for every x.~For ∃y∀x, a single fixed y would need to equal −x for all integers.~Taking x=0 and x=1 demands y=0 and y=−1 simultaneously; the second statement is false.''',
'Changing quantifier order changes meaning.||Negation must reach the predicate, not stop at swapping symbols.',
'¬∀ = ∃¬ and ¬∃ = ∀¬||State the domain before choosing witnesses.',
[('On domain {1,2,3}, how many witnesses satisfy ∃x: x²>4?',1,'Test the three values.','Only x=3 has x²>4; x=2 gives equality, not strict inequality.'),('For x,y in {0,1}, how many fixed values of y make ∀x: x+y=1 true?',0,'One y must work for both choices of x.','x=0 requires y=1 and x=1 requires y=0. No fixed y works.')])
L(0,'sets-functions','Sets, relations and functions','Sets;Relations;Functions;Equivalence relations','Logic and quantifiers.',
'Sets describe collections, relations describe permitted pairs, and functions choose one output for every input. Keep those three objects separate: a function is a special relation, but a general relation may connect an element to many others or none.',
'''Sets and counting::A∪B includes elements in either set; A∩B includes elements in both; A−B removes B from A. A power set contains every subset, so |P(A)|=2^|A|. The Cartesian product A×B consists of ordered pairs and has |A|×|B| elements for finite sets. Inclusion–exclusion gives |A∪B|=|A|+|B|−|A∩B|.
||Properties of relations::On A, reflexivity means aRa for every a. Symmetry means aRb implies bRa. Antisymmetry means aRb and bRa imply a=b. Transitivity means aRb and bRc imply aRc. Reflexive+symmetric+transitive defines an equivalence relation, whose equivalence classes partition A. Test the properties individually; loops and pair direction matter.
||Functions and inverses::A function A→B is total and single-valued. Injectivity forbids two distinct inputs sharing an output; surjectivity requires every codomain element to be reached. Bijectivity combines both and permits a two-sided inverse. On finite sets of equal size, injectivity and surjectivity are equivalent; that shortcut depends on finiteness and equal cardinality.''',
'''Partition integers by congruence modulo 3::aRb iff 3 divides a−b. a−a=0 establishes reflexivity.~Reversing a−b changes its sign, preserving divisibility; adding two divisible differences establishes transitivity.~The classes are residues 0,1,2, so the relation is an equivalence relation.
||Count functions from a 3-element set to a 2-element set::Each input independently selects one of two outputs: 2³=8 total functions.~A nonsurjective function uses only one output; there are two constant functions.~Thus 8−2=6 functions are surjective. None is injective, by the pigeonhole principle.''',
'Antisymmetric does not mean “not symmetric”; identity relations satisfy both.||The codomain is part of a function definition; changing it can change surjectivity.',
'Number of functions from m inputs to n outputs is nᵐ.||Equivalence classes are disjoint and cover the underlying set.',
[('A set has 5 elements. How many subsets does it have?',32,'Each element is included or excluded.','There are 2⁵=32 subsets, including the empty set and the full set.'),('How many functions exist from a 2-element set to a 3-element set?',9,'Choose an output independently for each input.','3 choices for the first input times 3 for the second gives 9.')])
L(0,'orders-lattices','Partial orders and lattices','Partial orders;Hasse diagrams;Lattices','Relations; divisibility and subsets.',
'An order need not compare every pair. A dependency graph of tasks can say one task comes before another while leaving independent tasks incomparable. A lattice adds a reliable way to combine any two elements using their closest common upper and lower bounds.',
'''Partial order and diagrams::A partial order is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive. If every pair is comparable, it is a total order. A Hasse diagram omits loops and edges implied by transitivity; lower-to-higher paths imply the order. An edge represents a cover relation: no element lies strictly between its endpoints.
||Extremal elements::A least element is below every element and is unique if it exists; a minimal element has no strictly smaller element and several may exist. Greatest and maximal are dual. An upper bound of a pair lies above both; the least upper bound is the smallest among those bounds, not just any convenient upper bound.
||Lattice operations::A lattice requires every pair to have a unique least upper bound (join) and greatest lower bound (meet). In a power-set lattice ordered by inclusion, join is union and meet is intersection. For positive divisors of an integer ordered by divisibility, join is lcm and meet is gcd. A finite lattice has top and bottom elements.''',
'''Find meet and join of 4 and 6 among divisors of 12::Common divisors are 1 and 2; the greatest under divisibility is 2.~Common multiples inside the set include 12; lcm(4,6)=12.~Thus meet=2 and join=12. Numerical minimum and maximum would have given the wrong answer.
||Identify minimal versus least in {2,3,6}, ordered by divisibility::2 and 3 have no smaller element in the set, so both are minimal.~Neither divides the other, so neither is below every element.~There is no least element; 6 is greatest because both 2 and 3 divide it.''',
'Do not draw all transitive edges in a Hasse diagram.||Minimal does not imply least.',
'Partial order = reflexive + antisymmetric + transitive.||Join is least upper bound; meet is greatest lower bound.',
[('In the divisors-of-12 lattice, what is the meet of 4 and 6?',2,'Use gcd, not ordinary minimum.','gcd(4,6)=2, the greatest common lower bound under divisibility.'),('How many elements are in the power-set lattice of a 3-element set?',8,'All subsets are nodes.','There are 2³=8 subsets.')])
L(0,'algebraic-structures','Monoids and groups','Monoids;Groups;Identity;Inverses','Sets, functions and binary operations.',
'An algebraic structure is a set together with an operation and laws. Start with closure: combining allowed objects must produce another allowed object. Additional laws let you reorganize computations, introduce an empty operation, or undo an operation.',
'''Semigroup and monoid::A binary operation maps S×S into S. Associativity says (a*b)*c=a*(b*c) and permits dropping parentheses. A semigroup is closed and associative. A monoid additionally has an identity e with e*a=a*e=a. Strings under concatenation form a monoid; the empty string is the identity. Concatenation is generally not commutative.
||Group axioms::A group is a monoid in which every element a has an inverse a⁻¹ satisfying a*a⁻¹=a⁻¹*a=e. Commutativity is not required; when it holds, the group is abelian. Integers under addition form an abelian group; nonnegative integers under addition form only a monoid because positive values have no additive inverse there.
||Modular examples and uniqueness::Residues modulo n under addition form a group with identity 0 and inverse n−a modulo n. Under multiplication, only residues relatively prime to n have modular inverses. Identities and inverses are unique in a group: apply the identity laws or associativity to show any two candidates must coincide.''',
'''Is multiplication on {1,2,3,4} modulo 5 a group?::Products of nonzero residues remain nonzero modulo prime 5; associativity is inherited.~Identity is 1; inverses are 1↔1, 2↔3, 4↔4.~All axioms hold, and multiplication is commutative, so it is an abelian group.
||Find the additive inverse of 5 modulo 8::We need 5+x ≡ 0 mod 8.~x=3 gives 5+3=8 ≡ 0.~The identity is 0, not 1, because the operation is addition.''',
'Always specify both the set and operation.||A multiplicative inverse modulo composite n does not exist for every nonzero residue.',
'Monoid = associative operation + identity.||Group = monoid + inverse for every element.',
[('What is the additive inverse of 4 modulo 7, expressed between 0 and 6?',3,'Solve 4+x ≡ 0 mod 7.','4+3=7, congruent to zero.'),('What is the multiplicative inverse of 3 modulo 7, expressed between 0 and 6?',5,'Solve 3x ≡ 1 mod 7.','3×5=15 ≡ 1 modulo 7.')])
L(0,'graphs-matching','Graph connectivity, matching and colouring','Graphs;Connectivity;Matching;Colouring','Sets and elementary counting.',
'Graphs model pairwise connections. Connectivity is about reachability, matching allocates disjoint pairs, and colouring separates conflicting neighbours. A question can involve the same drawing but demand a completely different invariant, so identify the task before computing.',
'''Degrees and connectivity::For an undirected graph, the sum of vertex degrees is 2E because every edge has two endpoints. A path joins vertices; connected components are maximal connected regions. A connected acyclic undirected graph is a tree with V−1 edges. Removing a bridge increases component count; removing an articulation vertex can disconnect the graph.
||Matching::A matching is a set of edges sharing no endpoints. A maximum matching has the largest cardinality; a maximal matching merely cannot be enlarged by adding an edge. In a bipartite graph with parts X,Y, Hall’s theorem gives a matching saturating X iff every subset S of X satisfies |N(S)|≥|S|. Check deficient subsets rather than just individual degrees.
||Colouring::A proper vertex colouring assigns different colours to adjacent vertices. The minimum number is the chromatic number. A complete graph Kₙ needs n colours. A graph is bipartite iff it has no odd cycle; any nonempty bipartite graph with an edge needs two colours. Colouring disconnected components can reuse colours.''',
'''Analyse the cycle C₅::Five vertices each have degree 2, so the degree sum is 10 and there are 5 edges.~The largest matching has floor(5/2)=2 edges because each edge consumes two vertices; such a matching exists.~An odd cycle is not bipartite and needs 3 colours; alternating two colours fails at the closing edge.
||Use Hall’s condition::Let X={a,b,c}, Y={1,2,3}; a and b connect only to 1, while c connects to 2 and 3.~For S={a,b}, the neighbour set has only one element.~|N(S)|=1<2 proves there is no matching saturating X, even though every vertex of X has a neighbour.''',
'Maximal and maximum are different optimization concepts.||A directed graph requires specifying weak or strong connectivity.',
'Undirected degree sum = 2E.||Tree edges = V−1.||Bipartite ⇔ no odd cycle.',
[('What is the chromatic number of a cycle with 7 vertices?',3,'Odd cycles cannot use two colours.','Alternate two colours along six vertices and use a third for the final vertex; at least three are needed.'),('An undirected graph has degree sum 26. How many edges does it have?',13,'Each edge contributes twice.','E=26/2=13.')])
L(0,'counting','Counting, inclusion–exclusion and pigeonhole','Counting;Permutations;Combinations;Inclusion–exclusion','Sets; factorial notation.',
'Counting is most reliable when you describe a construction of the object. Ask whether order matters, repetition is allowed, and restrictions are independent. Formulas follow those choices; picking a formula before interpreting the object is a common source of errors.',
'''Sum and product rules::Use addition for disjoint alternative cases and multiplication for successive independent choices. Ordered selections without replacement are n!/(n−r)!; unordered selections divide by r!, giving C(n,r). If ordered positions can repeat symbols from an alphabet of size n, there are nʳ assignments.
||Restrictions and overcounting::For at least one occurrence, count all objects minus those with none. Inclusion–exclusion adds single-set counts, subtracts pair overlaps, and adds triple overlaps. Stars and bars counts nonnegative integer solutions x₁+…+xₖ=n as C(n+k−1,k−1); for positive solutions first subtract one from each variable.
||Pigeonhole reasoning::Putting more than k objects into k boxes forces some box to contain at least two. The generalized bound is at least ceiling(n/k) objects in one box. Define the boxes clearly: residues modulo an integer, months, or possible degree values. The principle guarantees existence but usually does not identify the object.''',
'''Choose a committee of 3 from 8 people::Committee order is irrelevant and nobody is repeated.~Ordered selection counts 8×7×6=336, but each committee appears 3!=6 times.~Divide to get 336/6=56 committees.
||Count length-4 binary strings containing at least one 1::There are 2⁴=16 total strings.~The forbidden case has no 1, so every position must be zero; exactly one string.~Subtract to obtain 15. Choosing a position for the first 1 without case care can double-count.''',
'Divide by r! only when every unordered object was counted exactly r! times.||At least and exactly require different case logic.',
'Ordered without replacement: P(n,r).||Unordered: C(n,r).||Nonnegative k-part sum: C(n+k−1,k−1).',
[('How many 3-person committees can be chosen from 6 people?',20,'Use an unordered selection.','C(6,3)=6×5×4/(3×2×1)=20.'),('How many nonnegative integer solutions satisfy x+y+z=4?',15,'Apply stars and bars with n=4,k=3.','C(4+3−1,3−1)=C(6,2)=15.')])
L(0,'recurrences','Recurrence relations and generating functions','Recurrence relations;Generating functions','Counting; geometric series; simple algebra.',
'A recurrence expresses a large object in terms of smaller ones, but base cases determine which sequence it describes. A generating function turns an entire sequence into one formal algebraic object; coefficients store the answers while multiplication represents convolution.',
'''Solving recurrences::First compute a few terms and record initial values. For aₙ=c aₙ₋₁, repeated substitution gives aₙ=cⁿa₀. For aₙ=r aₙ₋₁+b, expand a geometric sum. Linear homogeneous recurrences with constant coefficients use characteristic roots: aₙ=u aₙ₋₁+v aₙ₋₂ yields λ²−uλ−v=0. Distinct roots give Aλ₁ⁿ+Bλ₂ⁿ; repeated roots need a factor n.
||Generating-function identities::Define A(x)=Σₙ≥₀ aₙxⁿ. The constant sequence 1 has generating function 1/(1−x). Differentiate the geometric series formally to obtain Σnxⁿ=x/(1−x)². An index shift requires accounting for omitted initial terms: Σₙ≥₁aₙ₋₁xⁿ=xA(x).
||Derivation from a recurrence::Multiply both sides by xⁿ and sum over the indices for which the recurrence holds. Isolate A(x), keeping boundary terms. Expand the resulting rational function or use known identities to recover coefficients. This method also models counting when choices concatenate and coefficient sums represent total size.''',
'''Solve aₙ=2aₙ₋₁+1 with a₀=0::Expand: aₙ=2(2aₙ₋₂+1)+1=4aₙ₋₂+3.~Continuing gives aₙ=2ⁿa₀+Σᵢ₌₀ⁿ⁻¹2ⁱ.~The geometric sum is 2ⁿ−1, so aₙ=2ⁿ−1; check a₁=1 and a₂=3.
||Find the generating function of aₙ=2n+3::Split the sum into 2Σnxⁿ+3Σxⁿ.~Substitute identities: 2x/(1−x)²+3/(1−x).~Use a common denominator to obtain (3−x)/(1−x)².''',
'Never drop initial values when shifting an index.||A repeated characteristic root needs an extra n factor.',
'Σxⁿ=1/(1−x); Σnxⁿ=x/(1−x)².||Check any closed form against base cases.',
[('For a₀=1 and aₙ=3aₙ₋₁, what is a₄?',81,'Repeatedly multiply by 3.','a₄=3⁴×1=81.'),('For a₀=0 and aₙ=2aₙ₋₁+1, what is a₅?',31,'Use the derived geometric-sum result.','a₅=2⁵−1=31.')])
L(0,'matrices-rank','Matrices, determinants and linear systems','Matrices;Determinants;System of linear equations;Rank','Basic algebra and simultaneous equations.',
'A matrix is a compact representation of a linear transformation or system. Row reduction exposes independent constraints. Rank counts independent directions, not the number of rows; redundant equations cannot determine additional unknowns.',
'''Matrix operations and determinants::An m×n matrix multiplies an n×p matrix to give m×p; multiplication is generally noncommutative. For a 2×2 matrix [[a,b],[c,d]], det=ad−bc. Swapping rows reverses determinant sign; scaling a row scales determinant; adding a multiple of another row leaves it unchanged. A square matrix is invertible iff its determinant is nonzero.
||Elimination and rank::Gaussian elimination uses elementary row operations to form echelon shape. Each pivot corresponds to an independent row and column direction; rank is the number of pivots. A zero row in the coefficient matrix may simply be redundancy, while a row [0 … 0 | nonzero] in the augmented matrix is a contradiction.
||Classifying solutions::For Ax=b, consistency requires rank(A)=rank([A|b]). If consistent and rank(A)=n unknowns, the solution is unique. If rank<n, there are n−rank free variables and infinitely many real solutions. The homogeneous system Ax=0 is always consistent and has nonzero solutions exactly when rank<n.''',
'''Solve x+y=3 and 2x+2y=6::The second equation is twice the first, so elimination creates a zero row.~Rank(A)=rank([A|b])=1 with two unknowns.~Let y=t; x=3−t. There are infinitely many solutions, not two independent equations.
||Determine invertibility of [[1,2],[3,4]]::Compute det=1×4−2×3=−2.~Nonzero determinant gives full rank 2 and a unique solution for every right-hand side.~The inverse is (−1/2)[[4,−2],[−3,1]]; multiplying verifies identity.''',
'Count pivots rather than nonzero-looking original rows.||An inconsistent augmented row is not the same as a redundant zero row.',
'Consistent iff rank(A)=rank([A|b]).||Nullity = number of columns − rank.',
[('A consistent system has 5 unknowns and rank 3. How many free variables are there?',2,'Subtract rank from unknown count.','Nullity=5−3=2.'),('What is det([[2,1],[4,3]])?',2,'Use ad−bc.','2×3−1×4=2.')])
L(0,'eigen-lu','Eigenvalues, eigenvectors and LU decomposition','Eigenvalues;Eigenvectors;LU decomposition','Matrices, determinants and elimination.',
'An eigenvector is a direction a linear transformation stretches without rotating it away from that line. LU decomposition instead records the elimination process so many systems sharing the same coefficient matrix can be solved cheaply.',
'''Finding eigenspaces::Av=λv means (A−λI)v=0 with v≠0. A nonzero solution requires det(A−λI)=0, the characteristic equation. For each root λ, row-reduce A−λI to find its nullspace. Eigenvectors can be scaled by nonzero constants; zero is never an eigenvector.
||Useful checks and diagonalization::The eigenvalue sum equals trace and product equals determinant, counting algebraic multiplicity. Triangular matrices have diagonal entries as eigenvalues. A matrix is diagonalizable if it has enough linearly independent eigenvectors. A repeated eigenvalue does not alone prevent diagonalization, but its eigenspace may be too small.
||LU solving::Gaussian elimination can factor A=LU, with L lower triangular and U upper triangular. Multipliers are stored in L. Solve Ly=b by forward substitution and Ux=y by backward substitution. If elimination needs row swaps, use a permutation matrix: PA=LU. Factor once, then solve repeated right-hand sides without repeating elimination.''',
'''Find eigenvalues of [[2,1],[0,3]]::The characteristic determinant is (2−λ)(3−λ).~Roots are 2 and 3, matching the diagonal entries.~For λ=2, v₂=0; for λ=3, v₂=v₁. Directions (1,0) and (1,1) are independent.
||Factor [[2,1],[4,3]]::Eliminate the 4 using multiplier 4/2=2: second row minus twice first gives [0,1].~U=[[2,1],[0,1]], and L=[[1,0],[2,1]].~Multiplying LU gives [[2,1],[4,3]], confirming the factorization.''',
'An eigenvalue of zero implies singularity, but zero is not an eigenvector.||Do not ignore permutation matrices when a pivot is zero.',
'Av=λv; det(A−λI)=0.||trace = eigenvalue sum; determinant = product.||PA=LU with pivoting.',
[('A 2×2 matrix has trace 7 and one eigenvalue 2. What is its other eigenvalue?',5,'Eigenvalues sum to the trace.','2+λ=7, so λ=5.'),('For eigenvalues 3 and −2, what is the determinant?',-6,'Multiply the eigenvalues.','3×(−2)=−6.')])
L(0,'limits-continuity','Limits, continuity and differentiability','Limits;Continuity;Differentiability','Functions and algebra.',
'A limit describes nearby behaviour, while a function value describes the point itself. Continuity joins those ideas. Differentiability asks for one consistent local slope, a stronger requirement than having no break in the graph.',
'''Evaluating limits::A two-sided limit exists when left and right limits agree. Try substitution first; an indeterminate 0/0 asks for simplification, not an answer. Factor and cancel on a punctured neighbourhood, rationalize radicals, or use standard limits such as sin x/x→1 in radians. Cancellation does not redefine the function at the removed point.
||Continuity::At a, require f(a) to be defined, limₓ→ₐf(x) to exist, and both to agree. For piecewise functions check both sides and the assigned point value. A removable discontinuity can be repaired by defining the missing value to equal the limit; jump discontinuities cannot be repaired by one point assignment.
||Derivative from definition::f′(a)=limₕ→₀[f(a+h)−f(a)]/h. Left and right derivative limits must agree. Differentiability implies continuity because the increment equals h times a bounded difference quotient near the point. Continuity does not imply differentiability: a corner can have conflicting one-sided slopes.''',
'''Evaluate limₓ→₂ (x²−4)/(x−2)::Direct substitution gives 0/0, so factor x²−4=(x−2)(x+2).~For x≠2 the quotient equals x+2.~Its limit is 4. The original function remains undefined at 2 unless explicitly extended.
||Check differentiability of |x| at 0::The function approaches zero from both sides and equals zero, so it is continuous.~For h>0, |h|/h=1; for h<0, it equals −1.~The derivative does not exist because the slopes disagree.''',
'0/0 is an indeterminate form, not zero.||Continuity alone does not permit differentiation.',
'Continuity needs limit=value.||Differentiability ⇒ continuity, not conversely.',
[('What is limₓ→₃ (x²−9)/(x−3)?',6,'Factor a difference of squares.','For x≠3, the ratio is x+3; the limit is 6.'),('What value at x=0 makes sin(x)/x continuous there?',1,'Use the standard radian limit.','The limit is 1, so define f(0)=1.')])
L(0,'extrema-mvt','Derivatives, extrema and the mean value theorem','Maxima and minima;Mean value theorem;Differentiation','Limits and differentiability.',
'Derivatives reveal change, but a zero slope alone does not identify a maximum. The mean value theorem connects average change over an interval to an instantaneous slope inside it, under precise continuity and differentiability assumptions.',
'''Derivative rules::Differentiate sums termwise; the product rule is (uv)′=u′v+uv′, not u′v′. The chain rule multiplies outer derivative by inner derivative. For a quotient use (u′v−uv′)/v² where v≠0. These rules follow from the difference quotient and simplify examining polynomial and composite functions.
||Finding extrema::On a closed interval, a continuous function attains absolute extrema. List endpoints and interior critical points where derivative is zero or undefined; compare function values. A sign change from + to − in f′ gives a local maximum, from − to + a local minimum. If f′(c)=0, positive f″ gives a local minimum and negative f″ gives a maximum; f″=0 is inconclusive.
||Rolle and mean value theorems::If f is continuous on [a,b], differentiable on (a,b), and f(a)=f(b), Rolle gives f′(c)=0 inside. The MVT removes the equal-endpoint requirement and gives f′(c)=[f(b)−f(a)]/(b−a). Verify assumptions before solving the slope equation; functions with corners can violate the conclusion.''',
'''Find extrema of x²−4x+1 on [0,5]::Derivative 2x−4 vanishes at x=2, which lies inside the interval.~Evaluate f(0)=1, f(2)=−3, f(5)=6.~Minimum is −3 at 2 and maximum is 6 at 5; checking only the critical point misses the maximum.
||Apply MVT to x² on [1,3]::The polynomial is continuous and differentiable, so assumptions hold.~Average slope=(9−1)/(3−1)=4.~Set f′(c)=2c=4, obtaining c=2 inside the interval.''',
'f′=0 can be a stationary inflection, e.g. x³ at zero.||Do not omit endpoints for absolute extrema.',
'MVT: f′(c)=(f(b)−f(a))/(b−a).||Check derivative signs or actual values; zero slope is only a candidate.',
[('At which x does x²−6x+2 attain its minimum over the real numbers?',3,'Set the derivative to zero and inspect curvature.','2x−6=0 gives x=3, and f″=2>0 confirms a minimum.'),('For f(x)=x² on [0,4], what MVT point c satisfies the theorem?',2,'Find the secant slope.','Average slope=16/4=4; 2c=4 gives c=2.')])
L(0,'integration','Integration and accumulated quantities','Integration;Definite integrals;Substitution;Integration by parts','Differentiation and algebra.',
'Integration sums contributions over a continuum. A definite integral is signed accumulation; area below the axis contributes negatively. An antiderivative reverses differentiation, but definite integrals become useful only after applying limits consistently.',
'''Fundamental theorem and basic rules::If f is continuous, an antiderivative F satisfies ∫ₐᵇf(x)dx=F(b)−F(a). The indefinite integral includes an arbitrary constant C. For n≠−1, ∫xⁿdx=xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1)+C; ∫1/x dx=ln|x|+C on intervals avoiding zero. Definite integral limits have orientation: reversing them changes the sign.
||Substitution and parts::For a composite expression, choose u=g(x) and du=g′(x)dx. In definite integrals, either transform both limits into u or substitute back before using original limits. Integration by parts is ∫u dv=uv−∫v du and is useful for products such as x eˣ or x ln x. Choose a differentiable part that simplifies.
||Symmetry and improper cases::On [−a,a], an odd function integrates to zero and an even function integrates to twice the integral on [0,a]. This requires an integrable function; a singularity cannot be dismissed just by symmetry. For improper integrals use one-sided limits at singularities or infinity and require convergence.''',
'''Compute ∫₀² 3x² dx::An antiderivative is x³.~Evaluate 2³−0³=8.~The integrand is nonnegative, so the signed integral also equals geometric area.
||Compute ∫₀¹ 2x e^(x²) dx::Choose u=x², so du=2x dx.~Transform the limits: x=0 gives u=0; x=1 gives u=1.~The integral becomes ∫₀¹eᵘdu=e−1. No stray x remains after substitution.''',
'Definite integrals do not retain an arbitrary +C.||Do not mix original limits with a transformed variable.',
'∫ₐᵇf = F(b)−F(a).||Substitution changes differential and limits.||Odd symmetry works only when the integral exists.',
[('What is ∫₀³ 2x dx?',9,'Use antiderivative x².','3²−0²=9.'),('What is ∫₋₂² x³ dx?',0,'Use odd symmetry on a finite interval.','x³ is odd and continuous, so equal negative and positive contributions cancel.')])
L(0,'conditional-bayes','Probability, conditional probability and Bayes','Conditional probability;Bayes theorem;Independence','Sets and counting.',
'A conditional probability changes the population under consideration. Once B is known, count only outcomes inside B. Bayes reverses a conditioning direction by combining prior frequencies with evidence; a high detection rate need not imply a high probability that a detected condition is real.',
'''Events and conditioning::A probability measure assigns nonnegative values totaling 1 to the sample space. For P(B)>0, P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B). The multiplication rule gives P(A∩B)=P(A|B)P(B). Mutually exclusive nonzero-probability events cannot be independent, because their intersection is empty.
||Total probability and Bayes::If B₁,…,Bₖ form a partition, P(A)=ΣP(A|Bᵢ)P(Bᵢ). Bayes gives P(Bⱼ|A)=P(A|Bⱼ)P(Bⱼ)/P(A). Construct the denominator from every way the evidence can occur. Use a frequency table when fractions feel abstract: population counts often make false positives obvious.
||Independence::A and B are independent iff P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B), equivalently P(A|B)=P(A) when defined. Pairwise independence among three events is weaker than mutual independence: the triple intersection must also factor. Do not infer independence from different-looking descriptions.''',
'''A test has sensitivity 90%, false-positive rate 10%, and prevalence 1%. Find P(condition|positive)::Imagine 10000 people: 100 have the condition, and 90 of them test positive.~Of 9900 without it, 990 test positive.~Positive tests total 1080; the posterior is 90/1080=1/12≈8.33%, not 90%.
||Draw two cards without replacement; find probability both are aces::First draw is an ace with probability 4/52.~Given that, only 3 aces among 51 cards remain.~Multiply (4/52)(3/51)=1/221. Treating the draws as independent would use the wrong second factor.''',
'P(A|B) is usually not P(B|A).||Disjointness and independence are different.',
'Bayes = likelihood × prior / evidence.||Without replacement usually creates dependence.',
[('If P(A∩B)=0.12 and P(B)=0.3, what is P(A|B)?',0.4,'Restrict to B and divide.','0.12/0.3=0.4.'),('Independent events have probabilities 0.2 and 0.5. What is their intersection probability?',0.1,'Multiply independent probabilities.','0.2×0.5=0.1.')])
L(0,'random-statistics','Random variables and descriptive statistics','Random variables;Mean;Median;Mode;Standard deviation','Probability and basic summation.',
'A random variable attaches a number to an outcome. The distribution tells you how often values occur; expectation, variance, and quantiles summarize different aspects. An average alone cannot tell you whether values are tightly concentrated or widely spread.',
'''PMF, PDF and CDF::For discrete X, p(x)=P(X=x) is a probability mass function and its values sum to 1. For continuous X, a density f integrates to 1; probabilities are areas, so P(X=x)=0 at individual points. The cumulative distribution F(x)=P(X≤x) works in both cases and is nondecreasing.
||Expectation and dispersion::For discrete X, E[X]=Σxp(x); for continuous X, integrate xf(x). E[aX+b]=aE[X]+b without needing independence. Var(X)=E[X²]−E[X]² and Var(aX+b)=a²Var(X). Standard deviation is the square root of variance. Variances of sums add for independent variables; otherwise covariance terms matter.
||Mean, median and mode::The arithmetic mean uses every observation and is sensitive to outliers. Median splits the ordered data into halves and resists extreme values. Mode is a most frequent value and need not be unique. Distinguish population variance, with divisor n, from the conventional unbiased sample variance, with divisor n−1, when the question specifies a sample estimator.''',
'''Compute moments when X is 0 or 2 with equal probability::E[X]=0(1/2)+2(1/2)=1.~E[X²]=0+4(1/2)=2.~Variance=2−1²=1 and standard deviation=1.
||Summarize the data 1,2,2,3,12::Sum=20 and count=5, so mean=4.~Sorted middle value is 2, so median=2; 2 also occurs most often, so mode=2.~The large observation shifts the mean away from the typical central values.''',
'A PDF value is a density, not a point probability.||E[X²] is not generally E[X]².',
'Var(X)=E[X²]−E[X]².||Var(aX+b)=a²Var(X).||CDF=P(X≤x).',
[('For X equal to 1 or 3 with probability 1/2 each, what is E[X]?',2,'Take the weighted average.','(1+3)/2=2.'),('If Var(X)=4, what is Var(3X+7)?',36,'A shift does not affect variance.','3²×4=36.')])
L(0,'binomial-poisson','Binomial and Poisson distributions','Binomial distribution;Poisson distribution','Random variables; independent trials; combinations.',
'The binomial distribution counts successes in a fixed number of independent identical trials. Poisson models event counts in a window with a specified mean rate. Identify the experiment first: fixed trials and success probability suggest binomial; an event rate over time suggests Poisson.',
'''Binomial construction::X~Binomial(n,p) has P(X=k)=C(n,k)pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ. The combination chooses success positions; multiplying gives the probability of a specific ordering. E[X]=np and Var(X)=np(1−p). At least one success is conveniently 1−(1−p)ⁿ.
||Poisson counts::X~Poisson(λ) has P(X=k)=e^(−λ)λᵏ/k!, k≥0. Its mean and variance are both λ. If the rate is r per unit time, a duration t has λ=rt under the homogeneous Poisson-process model. Independent Poisson counts add to a Poisson count with summed means.
||When approximation is sensible::For many independent trials with small p and moderate np, Binomial(n,p) can be approximated by Poisson(np). It is an approximation, not a rule replacing all binomial questions. Check the event and units; changing a 1-minute window to 3 minutes multiplies λ by three, not p by three.''',
'''Fair coin tossed four times: exactly two heads::Use n=4,p=1/2,k=2.~Choose the head positions in C(4,2)=6 ways.~Each ordering has probability (1/2)⁴=1/16, so the probability is 6/16=3/8.
||Calls arrive with rate 2 per minute. Find mean and zero-call probability in 3 minutes::The window mean is λ=2×3=6.~Expected count is 6 and variance is 6.~P(X=0)=e⁻⁶6⁰/0!=e⁻⁶; the duration must be included before using the formula.''',
'Binomial trials need the same p and independence.||Poisson λ belongs to the actual time window.',
'Binomial: mean np, variance np(1−p).||Poisson: mean=variance=λ.',
[('For Binomial(10,0.3), what is the mean?',3,'Use np.','10×0.3=3.'),('A Poisson process has rate 4 per hour. What is the count variance over 2 hours?',8,'Compute the window mean first.','λ=4×2=8; Poisson variance equals λ.')])
L(0,'continuous-distributions','Uniform, exponential and normal distributions','Uniform distribution;Normal distribution;Exponential distribution','Densities, integration, expectation.',
'Continuous probabilities are interval areas. Uniform gives equal density over a bounded interval; exponential models waiting time with a constant hazard; normal describes symmetric variation around a mean. Each has a different support and interpretation.',
'''Uniform[a,b]::The density is 1/(b−a) inside [a,b] and zero outside. An interval probability is its overlapping length divided by b−a. Mean=(a+b)/2 and variance=(b−a)²/12. Equal density does not mean a positive probability at each exact point; any one point still has probability zero.
||Exponential rate λ::The density is λe^(−λx) for x≥0; survival P(X>t)=e^(−λt). Mean=1/λ and variance=1/λ². Memorylessness follows because P(X>s+t|X>s)=e^(−λ(s+t))/e^(−λs)=e^(−λt). The rate parameter is the reciprocal of the mean, so check which parameterization the question uses.
||Normal(μ,σ²)::The density is symmetric around μ, so mean=median=mode=μ. Standardize using Z=(X−μ)/σ, not σ². Probabilities reduce to standard normal CDF Φ: P(a<X<b)=Φ((b−μ)/σ)−Φ((a−μ)/σ). Independent normal variables have normal sums with means and variances added.''',
'''For X~Uniform[2,8], find P(3<X<5)::Total interval length is 8−2=6.~Desired interval length is 5−3=2.~Probability=2/6=1/3; the density 1/6 alone is not the answer.
||Standardize X~Normal(10,4) at X=14::The second parameter is variance 4, so standard deviation is 2.~Z=(14−10)/2=2.~Thus P(X≤14)=Φ(2), rather than Φ(1).''',
'Normal notation typically gives variance, not standard deviation.||Exponential rate and scale are reciprocals.',
'Uniform probability = interval fraction.||Exponential survival=e^(−λt).||Z=(X−μ)/σ.',
[('For Uniform[0,10], what is P(2<X<7)?',0.5,'Compare interval lengths.','The desired interval has length 5 out of 10, so 0.5.'),('An exponential distribution has rate 0.25 per minute. What is its mean waiting time in minutes?',4,'Mean is reciprocal rate.','1/0.25=4 minutes.')])
